1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,248
This lesson covers all ten narrative sections in school 4B. De-
escalation is a communication strategy that may create time and
choice; it cannot guarantee that a dangerous situation becomes safe.

2
00:00:13,248 --> 00:00:22,320
We will keep three questions separate: what might be medically wrong,
what the behavior means to this person, and what is safe for the team
to do next.

3
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:40,752
A patient who is agitated may be experiencing a time-critical medical
problem. Begin with safety and an immediate impression, then look for
onset, breathing, temperature, injury, glucose risk, substances,
medications and other causes within your assessment and scope.

4
00:00:40,752 --> 00:00:54,768
Do not force a choice between “medical” and “behavioral.” A medical
condition can change behavior, and fear or trauma can coexist with
illness. Reassess when the story or observations change.

5
00:00:54,768 --> 00:01:07,512
Start with the whole scene, not a facial expression alone. Note
movement, speech, breathing, objects, crowding and exits. Compare with
what was known before you approached.

6
00:01:07,512 --> 00:01:21,864
Interpret the observations as working possibilities. Distress and an
active threat can coexist; a quiet patient can also have an urgent
medical problem. One category does not cancel the other questions.

7
00:01:21,864 --> 00:01:38,496
Choose the next action that fits the actual conditions. Safe
conversation may permit a focused question; an uncontrolled threat may
require distance and support before contact. Do not use a risk label
as an automatic treatment or detention order.

8
00:01:38,496 --> 00:01:50,736
Then check what changed. Increased space may help, have no effect or
reveal a new problem. Reassessment tests the plan instead of assuming
a technique worked because you used the right words.

9
00:01:50,736 --> 00:02:07,536
A sudden behavioral change, altered awareness, abnormal breathing,
collapse or extreme heat should raise medical concern. No single
observation identifies the cause, so combine history, focused exam and
repeated vital signs as safe.

10
00:02:07,536 --> 00:02:26,640
The school outline uses the phrase “Excited Delirium.” Treat that as
its syllabus wording only. Current teaching should describe observable
severe agitation and possible hyperactive delirium, identify causes,
and avoid using the phrase as a stand-alone diagnosis.

11
00:02:26,640 --> 00:03:02,016
Organize the oxygen chain into four functions: neural/chemical control
of breathing, the mechanical work of moving air, gas exchange and
transport, and distribution of pulmonary blood flow. Brain or
depressant-drug effects can impair drive; muscle weakness, trauma or
airway obstruction can impair movement. Alveoli need ventilation and
blood flow, and the blood needs adequate hemoglobin and circulation. A
behavioral change can therefore be a sign of a delivery problem, not
automatically a primary psychiatric symptom.

12
00:03:02,016 --> 00:03:45,000
Hypoxemia concerns low arterial oxygen, whereas tissue hypoxia
concerns inadequate oxygen delivery or use. SpO2 estimates a
hemoglobin saturation; PaO2 means arterial oxygen partial pressure and
reflects dissolved oxygen. Neither directly measures the whole
delivery chain. A person with severe anemia or poor circulation may
have a plausible saturation but insufficient oxygen delivery. Blood
gas analysis also informs carbon dioxide and acid-base status; it is
not a single saturation test. Do not rank SpO2 as universally superior
for determining oxygen content without considering hemoglobin.

13
00:03:45,000 --> 00:04:18,288
AVPU describes the response you actually elicited: alert, response to
voice, response only to an appropriate painful stimulus, or
unresponsive. Record the stimulus, response and time, not just sleepy
or confused. Alertness does not prove full orientation or decision-
making capacity, and response to voice is not equivalent to a normal
neurologic assessment. Use approved techniques without repeated
unnecessary painful stimulation, then look for a changing trend.

14
00:04:18,288 --> 00:04:49,584
The source AVPU table links decreasing responsiveness to airway
concern and a semi-prone position when injuries permit. Do not wait
for U before assessing breathing or assume every alert person protects
the airway despite vomiting, oral bleeding or injury. Positioning
depends on airway, breathing, trauma and the actual situation. If
ventilation is inadequate, provide the indicated support rather than
using a position or one consciousness letter as treatment by itself.

15
00:04:49,584 --> 00:05:06,312
Notice change from baseline and clusters of signs. Pacing may reflect
pain, fear, a need to move or a medical problem. Silence may be
processing, language access, distrust, exhaustion or a decision not to
answer.

16
00:05:06,312 --> 00:05:19,632
Use neutral language: “The person is pacing, sweating and speaking in
short phrases,” not “the person is crazy or dangerous.” Then ask what
the person wants and what changed.

17
00:05:19,632 --> 00:05:34,656
If fear is misread as aggression, crowding, commands or premature
coercion may intensify distress and damage trust. Silence, averted
eyes or a refused question do not establish intent to injure.

18
00:05:34,656 --> 00:05:49,248
The opposite error is ignoring specific danger because you want the
encounter to remain therapeutic. An assault, weapon or loss of safe
egress requires a changed safety plan, not a longer attempt to finish
the interview.

19
00:05:49,248 --> 00:06:03,288
A third error is explaining away altered awareness or collapse as bad
behaviour. Continue looking for medical causes and new deterioration
when assessment becomes safe; do not make the label do the work of an
examination.

20
00:06:03,288 --> 00:06:17,568
A useful team statement names the observation, concern and action: The
person moved into our exit route; I cannot confirm safe passage; we
need space and support. It does not claim certainty about the person’s
motive.

21
00:06:17,568 --> 00:06:31,344
When safe, create space, reduce the audience and remove unnecessary
stimulation. Avoid sudden touch, blocking exits, surrounding the
patient or making multiple people speak at once.

22
00:06:31,344 --> 00:06:42,408
Choose a position that protects both sides and preserves a route to
withdraw. Do not enter a confined or unsafe area simply because a calm
conversation seems possible.

23
00:06:42,408 --> 00:06:56,808
Ask whether sound, light or crowding is making the encounter harder.
Reduce unnecessary stimulation where feasible, but do not turn off
lighting needed for safe assessment or move into an unsafe secluded
space.

24
00:06:56,808 --> 00:07:09,576
Arrange one lead speaker and keep necessary staff connected. Invite
unneeded onlookers to create space without escalating a confrontation
or pretending you can control every person or camera.

25
00:07:09,576 --> 00:07:23,088
Protect dignity with an explanation, appropriate covering and the
minimum exposure required for assessment. Ask about a wanted support
person rather than automatically excluding or including all family.

26
00:07:23,088 --> 00:07:36,144
Reassess after the change. A quieter environment that blocks an exit,
separates the crew or hides breathing is not an improvement. Privacy
and safety must be planned together.

27
00:07:36,144 --> 00:07:48,360
A calm tone is helpful only if your position, pace and actions also
feel predictable. Do not imitate the person’s volume, issue rapid
commands or make a promise you cannot keep.

28
00:07:48,360 --> 00:08:01,728
Cultural humility still applies under pressure. Do not assume eye
contact, silence, distance or touch carries the same meaning for
everyone. State what you observed and check your interpretation.

29
00:08:01,728 --> 00:08:14,304
The school calls the calming influence of a regulated clinician co-
regulation. Treat this as a useful communication concept, not a
guarantee that your nervous system can control another person’s
response.

30
00:08:14,304 --> 00:08:29,616
Notice whether you are talking faster, interrupting or moving
abruptly. If safe, pause, ease your own breathing and use a lower,
steady voice. Do not tell a patient with breathing difficulty that
they simply need to relax.

31
00:08:29,616 --> 00:08:41,976
Ask your partner to observe exits and changes while one person leads
communication. Agree how a safety concern will be announced so that
the patient does not receive competing instructions.

32
00:08:41,976 --> 00:08:55,320
If your own frustration is impairing the encounter, acknowledge it to
the team and arrange an appropriate change of speaker or support. A
professional reset is more useful than trying to win the last word.

33
00:08:55,320 --> 00:09:07,416
Listen without rushing to correct. Empathy can sound like, “This feels
frightening and you want more space.” It does not mean agreeing that
another person has done something unverified.

34
00:09:07,416 --> 00:09:18,984
Ask one question at a time. Paraphrase the patient’s priority, then
summarize the next step in plain language. Check understanding instead
of treating a nod as proof.

35
00:09:18,984 --> 00:09:31,704
Listen for what the person is trying to protect or obtain. A demand to
leave may relate to a dependent person, a pet, a job or fear of the
service. Ask rather than guessing the reason.

36
00:09:31,704 --> 00:09:45,840
Allow a thought to finish when the situation permits. A brief pause
can be more helpful than immediately adding a solution. Continue
observing clinical and scene safety rather than becoming absorbed in
one story.

37
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:58,536
Empathize with the expressed difficulty: It sounds as though not
knowing the plan has made this harder. Invite correction. You can
acknowledge fear without agreeing that an unverified accusation is
true.

38
00:09:58,536 --> 00:10:10,368
Do not promise a painless procedure, immediate discharge or a
particular hospital outcome. Honest support explains what you can do
now and what remains outside your control.

39
00:10:10,368 --> 00:10:22,728
An open question gives room for the person’s priority. Once it is
clear, use focused questions for the immediate decision. Do not ask a
long list simply because it appears on your mental checklist.

40
00:10:22,728 --> 00:10:36,864
Explain the purpose of a potentially sensitive question and ask only
what is relevant. For example, an onset question can help distinguish
a new change from the person’s usual state without making a judgment
about character.

41
00:10:36,864 --> 00:10:48,048
Pause for the answer and use appropriate communication support.
Limited English, hearing loss or a long processing pause is not proof
of confusion or deliberate resistance.

42
00:10:48,048 --> 00:11:03,048
Urgent medical assessment may need a short direct question rather than
a broad conversation. Explain the change in priority. LEAPS organizes
communication; it does not require a complete interview before
responding to deterioration.

43
00:11:03,048 --> 00:11:13,992
Paraphrasing checks your interpretation of the concern. Use your own
words and invite correction: You want to know who will secure the shop
before we discuss leaving; have I understood that?

44
00:11:13,992 --> 00:11:27,288
A summary is broader. It states the concern, the immediate assessment
or support step, any genuine choice and what remains unresolved. It is
not merely repeating the patient’s last sentence.

45
00:11:27,288 --> 00:11:40,272
Do not insert an agreement that was never given. We have discussed
checking your breathing is different from You agreed to transport. A
response to one proposal does not automatically authorize the next
procedure.

46
00:11:40,272 --> 00:11:50,976
If the summary is corrected, update the plan rather than defending
your wording. The useful result is a shared next step, or a clear
description of what is still disputed.

47
00:11:50,976 --> 00:12:06,672
In this original case, a shopkeeper says everyone must leave and
repeatedly looks toward an unlocked rear door. From a safe position,
ask the main concern. They say a cash drawer and customer property
would be left unsecured.

48
00:12:06,672 --> 00:12:19,800
Acknowledge the concern without assuming it explains every symptom:
Leaving those items unsecured is worrying you. Ask whether there is a
person they want contacted while the immediate clinical assessment is
discussed.

49
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:31,224
Paraphrase the actual request and confirm permission before contacting
anyone. Do not search the premises, handle money or promise police
attendance as an improvised reassurance.

50
00:12:31,224 --> 00:12:45,864
Summarize the feasible next step and its limit. If new clinical danger
appears, explain that priority and obtain the indicated support. A
resolved practical concern is not proof that medical risk has
resolved.

51
00:12:45,864 --> 00:12:56,256
A choice is not meaningful if both options are unsafe or the decision
has already been made without explanation. Offer only options you can
honor, then follow through.

52
00:12:56,256 --> 00:13:07,560
When you must set a limit, be brief and concrete. Explain the safety
reason, say what you can do, and avoid threats or bargaining about
care that is required now.

53
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:20,064
De-escalation is not a requirement to remain close to an immediate
threat. If risk rises, stop the interaction, create distance,
communicate the hazard and request the appropriate help.

54
00:13:20,064 --> 00:13:35,928
Restraint or medication is not an automatic next step. It requires the
correct legal/service authority, trained personnel, monitoring and a
current protocol. This lesson does not teach hands-on restraint or
sedation technique.

55
00:13:35,928 --> 00:13:50,640
De-escalation may be failing when dangerous conduct intensifies or
safe passage is no longer available. Repeatedly refusing a question is
not the same evidence as a specific threat, so describe the actual
change.

56
00:13:50,640 --> 00:14:04,032
Do not remain to complete LEAPS, retrieve a replaceable bag or prove
that your technique works. Follow the trained egress plan with your
partner. Do not improvise a physical confrontation or pursue into
danger.

57
00:14:04,032 --> 00:14:19,296
From a safer position, tell dispatch where you are and why patient
contact cannot safely continue. Request the appropriate response and
communicate any known urgent patient concern. Do not assume somebody
else has reported the withdrawal.

58
00:14:19,296 --> 00:14:37,440
Continue coordinated responsibility: report, stage, reassess
conditions and arrange safe care through the incident response.
Withdrawal is not a reason to erase the patient from the plan, and an
apparent pause in hostility does not automatically clear re-entry.

59
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:49,968
The goal is not to win an argument or force cooperation. The goal is
to protect life and create a safe path to assessment. Do not promise
that a specific responder or legal outcome will occur.

60
00:14:49,968 --> 00:15:08,112
Under the BC Mental Health Act, section Twenty-Eight, subsection One
specifies an apprehension route for a police officer or constable
meeting its criteria. Do not teach this as independent PCP detention
authority or as the only legal route for all involuntary care.

61
00:15:08,112 --> 00:15:21,600
A patient can be distressed and still make a decision. A calm answer
also does not prove capacity. Neither a psychiatric label nor refusal
gives a clinician a new power to confine the person.

62
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:42,792
Section Twenty-Eight, subsection One of the BC Mental Health Act
applies to a police officer or constable satisfied, from observations
or received information, that the person is acting in a way likely to
endanger their own or others’ safety and apparently has a mental
disorder. It is not independent PCP detention authority.

63
00:15:42,792 --> 00:15:58,032
Do not expand that statement into a claim that every lawful
involuntary-care situation must begin with the same police route.
Other legal processes and emergency-care questions need their own
current authority and service consultation.

64
00:15:58,032 --> 00:16:14,160
For this lesson, the practical task is to communicate observations,
identify the authority question and obtain the appropriate decision.
LEAPS is not a consent test, certificate, restraint order or
substitute for the current refusal pathway.

65
00:16:14,160 --> 00:16:29,496
Prone positioning, pressure on the chest or neck, inability to see the
face, exhaustion and sudden quietness can signal severe danger. A
patient becoming still is not proof that the underlying problem has
resolved.

66
00:16:29,496 --> 00:16:46,776
The Week 10 outline asks learners to recall q5min vital signs and
respiratory monitoring for restraint scenarios. Keep that as a school
station target; follow the current authorized service protocol and
monitor continuously enough to detect deterioration.

67
00:16:46,776 --> 00:16:59,856
A patient may become quieter because communication helped, because
exhaustion increased or because their clinical condition deteriorated.
Determine what changed instead of treating quietness as a success
measure.

68
00:16:59,856 --> 00:17:12,672
Keep the face, breathing and response observable within the authorized
safety plan. The school’s five-minute vital-sign recall target does
not mean waiting five minutes to check a new loss of response.

69
00:17:12,672 --> 00:17:29,952
G01 emphasizes medical causes and close monitoring. Its level-specific
interventions and consultation requirements are not a general
permission to sedate or restrain. This lesson does not supply a
physical restraint technique or medication dose.

70
00:17:29,952 --> 00:17:44,712
During transfer, report the observed behaviour and clinical trend,
what was attempted, any intervention time and the response. Avoid
summarizing the entire event as calmed down when the team needs to
know about deterioration.

71
00:17:44,712 --> 00:17:59,112
A person at a transit terminal is pacing and shouting. A crowd is
filming. The person becomes unsteady and stops speaking. The new
quietness changes the problem; it is not an invitation to wait for
calm.

72
00:17:59,112 --> 00:18:12,264
Move the crowd back if safe, assign one communicator, maintain an
exit, check for immediate medical threats and summon appropriate
support. Do not approach alone or use the school label as a diagnosis.

73
00:18:12,264 --> 00:18:26,352
Labels can hide medical causes, intensify stigma and reduce the
usefulness of a handover. Record direct observations, the person’s
words when relevant, time course, interventions and response.

74
00:18:26,352 --> 00:18:38,160
A concise report says what you see, what you are concerned about, what
has been tried and what help is needed. State uncertainty honestly and
update the report as the patient changes.

75
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:50,856
Record observable behaviour, relevant words, clinical findings,
attempted communication and the reason for a changed plan. Separate
what you saw from another person’s report and from your
interpretation.

76
00:18:50,856 --> 00:19:04,584
After withdrawal, include the communication and staging timeline,
resources requested and the unresolved patient risk. Do not
retrospectively invent a threat or a refusal to make the record sound
tidier.

77
00:19:04,584 --> 00:19:20,424
In the operational review, compare the expected and actual effects of
one action. If multiple voices worsened confusion, a practical
improvement is one designated speaker and a partner safety role, not a
vague promise to be calmer.

78
00:19:20,424 --> 00:19:35,976
Use the applicable safety-reporting, supervisor and support pathways.
An operational debrief does not require personal trauma disclosure and
is not confidential therapy. Check readiness and equipment before
returning to duty.

79
00:19:35,976 --> 00:19:49,608
Use the course term “Excited Delirium” only when referring to the
school syllabus or an older source. In patient care and documentation,
describe the observed signs and use current service language.

80
00:19:49,608 --> 00:20:02,832
This lesson prepares your reasoning and communication. It does not
qualify you to restrain, detain, sedate or administer medication
outside your legal authority, training and current service policy.
